<%# ▲▼ — the fill order without JavaScript. Locals: `group`, `band`.

    TWO PLAIN FORMS, not two Stimulus actions, and that is the point of them: each button already
    carries the whole band in its new order as hidden `pool_ids[]` fields, so the reorder works
    with scripting off and is testable without synthesising drag events. reorder_controller does
    the same thing from the DOM when a card is dropped; both submit one shape to one endpoint.

    Named, not just glyphed: `Capybara.enable_aria_label` is on, and "Move Groceries up" is also
    what a screen reader has to hear — a button whose only text is ▲ says nothing about what
    moves or where.

    THE LIST BAKED IN HERE IS AS OLD AS THE PAGE, and that is the accepted cost of the two
    builders: a button pressed after a rule was added or deleted in another tab submits a set
    that is no longer the account's `in_fill_order` set, and `Pool.apply_fill_order` refuses it
    whole. The stale press writes nothing and says to reload, which is the same answer a stale
    drag gets — no half-order is reachable from either. %>
<span class="flex items-center gap-1">
  <% { -1 => ["▲", "up"], 1 => ["▼", "down"] }.each do |offset, (glyph, direction)| %>
    <%= button_to glyph,
                  budget_page_reorder_path,
                  method: :patch,
                  params: { pool_ids: reordered_pool_ids(band.groups, group, offset) },
                  disabled: reorder_edge?(band.groups, group, offset),
                  form_class: "inline-block leading-none",
                  aria: { label: "Move #{group.pool.name} #{direction}" },
                  title: "Move #{group.pool.name} #{direction}",
                  class: "px-1 text-xs rounded border border-gray-200 text-gray-500 " \
                         "enabled:hover:text-gray-900 enabled:hover:border-gray-400 " \
                         "disabled:text-gray-200 disabled:border-gray-100" %>
  <% end %>
</span>
